I fixed two of the three failed customers in this round and left the third alone because the judge's reason doesn't match its transcript. The fixes are saved as checkpoint 2, but neither has been re-run on the platform. The regression replay you asked for did not run: `fuzz_workflow.py --regression` again has no local copy of the bank's services. The checkpoint replay covered 0 recorded calls, so it shows no lost calls but proves nothing either way. The checkpoint passed the workflow check, selftest and 60 random model-free calls with no defects.

- **T-08 (passed 0 of 3, fixed):** the caller opened with "Kartıma fazla ödeme yaptım galiba, borcum ne durumda?". It was read as a dispute, and the call went straight to an agent before any debt was read. A dispute signal now counts as a request for the debt when no debt has been read yet, so the agent reads the card first. A caller who still says the amount is wrong after hearing it goes to an agent as before. The description of a dispute now says that just asking what the debt is, even after an overpayment, is not one. For this customer the reading should say the statement has no debt, never read the minus total, and route to the main menu.
- **T-10 (passed 2 of 3, fixed):** after "Dört iki..." the agent asked for the full number and repeated the whole four-digit question. The judge counted that as asking twice. A partial number now gets only "Lütfen kalan haneleri de söyler misiniz?", and the digits are still joined with the next part.
- **T-28 (passed 1 of 3, not changed):** the judge says the agent did not read the four digits one by one when asking for the last six. In the shown run the agent says "Son dört hanesi üç iki bir beş olan birden fazla kartınız var", which is digit by digit, the same form as the brief's "dört iki beş üç". If this keeps failing, the next thing to try is commas between the digits ("üç, iki, bir, beş"), but the brief doesn't ask for that.

